module tecs.sequence

--[[
Snapshot-safe programs, waits, ownership, actions, and tween timelines.

Sequences store control flow as data. A snapshot, a rewind, or a reload can
therefore preserve a playback's program, instruction pointer, waits, bindings,
and parameters. A coroutine cannot be serialized, so the sequencer compiles
authored steps into instructions and stores each playback's cursor, wait, and
branch as ordinary data. `nupp.tasks` owns the world's logical update; it
cannot own a playback that has to survive a save file.

## Programs and actions

Register an action on the world, name it from a program, and bind each playback
to the entities it acts on:

```nupp
tecs.sequence.registerAction(world, "game.lockControls", function(_world, _context): nil
    controlsLocked = true
end)

local intro <const> = tecs.sequence.define("game.bossIntro", {
    tecs.sequence.call("game.lockControls"),
    tecs.sequence.wait(1.5),
    tecs.sequence.emit("boss.ready"),
})

local playback <const> = tecs.sequence.play(world, intro, {owner = encounter, bindings = {boss = bossEntity}})
```

Program names are a snapshot compatibility surface. Use stable qualified names.
Redefining a name publishes a new version: running playbacks keep the version
they started on, and later `play` calls use the new one. Action bodies resolve
by name when they run, so a reload can replace action code without moving a
live instruction pointer.

Actions run synchronously and cannot suspend. An action that raises faults its
playback after preserving the mutations it already completed.

## Clocks and waits

`"fixed"` advances once per fixed step and serves deterministic gameplay.
`"frame"` advances once per gameplay frame and serves scripted input, which
needs exactly one decision per frame however many fixed steps that frame runs.
`"presentation"` advances once per frame carrying the frame's real elapsed
time, and serves values the simulation never reads.

Durations are seconds. Step and tick values count whole advances of the
program's clock. Every positive duration waits at least one tick, and
`waitSteps(0)` yields until the next tick and gives a loop a fresh instruction
budget.

`ecs.newWorld({nominalFrameTime = seconds})`, or `Application.Config.world`,
sets the duration used to convert frame and presentation waits to ticks. It
is positive and finite and defaults to 1/60 in both windowed and headless
sessions. `setNominalFrameTime(world, seconds)` changes future conversions in
that world only; already scheduled tick deadlines do not move. Restore a saved
playback into a world configured for its intended cadence.

`sourceKey(key, component, fields)` resolves a moving tween destination through
`World.byKey` on each evaluation, including after snapshot load or a new entity
claims the key. `TrackingTarget.key` does the same when its `entity` is zero.
An explicit entity id takes precedence and a stale id does not fall back to the
key. A missing source contributes zero, as other missing tracking sources do.

## Ownership

`owner` ties a playback to an entity, and despawning that entity cancels the
playback. A named `channel` gives one owner a replaceable slot, so starting a
second playback on the same channel cancels the first.

Pause uses named holders, and a playback resumes only after every holder
releases it. A branch inherits owner, bindings, parameters, budget, and pause
state, and cannot outlive its parent.

Each playback receives a bounded instruction budget per tick and faults with
`budgetExceeded` rather than hanging the frame.

## Timelines

A timeline compiles tweens into the same runtime and keeps the same ownership,
clock, channel, pause, and snapshot rules. Targets interpolate component
fields. A parallel block shares a start time, and a sequential block starts
after the one before it ends.

## Program construction

A sequence is written by calling the node constructors. The API has no generic
row decoder or shared data schema. A game that stores sequences as data decodes
its own schema into these constructors.
]]

local ecs = require("tecs.ecs")
local events = require("tecs.internal.events")
local data = require("tecs.data")
local sequencecursors = require("tecs.internal.sequencecursors")
local sequenceprogram = require("tecs.internal.sequenceprogram")
local sequenceregistry = require("tecs.internal.sequenceregistry")
local sequencesnapshot = require("tecs.internal.sequencesnapshot")
local sequencetypes = require("tecs.internal.sequencetypes")
local sequencevm = require("tecs.internal.sequencevm")
local tweeneval = require("tecs.internal.tweeneval")
local tweenrun = require("tecs.internal.tweenrun")

--- Explains why a cursor stopped running.
export type FaultReason = sequencetypes.FaultReason

--- Describes the lifecycle state of one playback.
export type PlaybackState = sequencetypes.PlaybackState

--- Describes the condition awaited by a `waitQuery` step.
export type QueryCondition = sequencetypes.QueryCondition

--- Reports how the playback awaited by `waitTween` ended.
export type TweenOutcome = sequencetypes.TweenOutcome

--- Selects the clock a program runs against.
export type ClockId = sequencetypes.ClockId

--- Represents a compiled, immutable program shared by every playback of it.
export type Program = sequencetypes.Program

--- Represents one authored step produced by a node constructor.
export type Node = sequencetypes.Node

--- References an entity supplied at `play` time.
export type EntityRef = sequencetypes.EntityRef

--- Identifies one playback through a generation-checked reference that remains
--- meaningful across a snapshot load.
export type Handle = sequencetypes.Handle

--- Configures `play`.
export type PlayOptions = sequencetypes.PlayOptions

--- Provides the context a registered action receives.
export type ActionContext = sequencetypes.ActionContext

--- Defines a registered effect that runs synchronously and cannot suspend.
export type Action = sequencetypes.Action

--- Defines the evaluator an `eval` step runs every tick.
export type Evaluator = sequencetypes.Evaluator

--- Defines the response an `await` step's provider must give.
export type Awaitable = sequencetypes.Awaitable

--- Reports a playback's current state.
export type Status = sequencetypes.Status

--- The payload an `emit` step delivers at address zero.
export type Emission = sequencetypes.Emission

--- Describes one step a playback reaches without branching.
export type Step = sequenceprogram.Step

--- Names a built-in easing curve.
export type EasingName = tweeneval.EasingName

--- Maps normalized input progress to eased output progress.
export type EasingFunction = tweeneval.EasingFunction

--- Names a built-in component-field target.
export type TargetName = tweeneval.TargetName

--- Describes the component and one to four numeric fields a timeline
--- operation writes.
export type Target = tweeneval.Target

--- Provides a live component-field tracking source.
export type TrackSource = tweeneval.TrackSource

--- Represents one authored timeline operation.
export type TimelineNode = tweeneval.TimelineNode

--- Selects how a timeline repeats.
export type PlaybackMode = tweeneval.PlaybackMode

--- Selects a dynamic tracking-source entity.
export type TrackingTarget = tweeneval.TrackingTarget

--- Configures `define`.
export type DefineOptions = {clock: ClockId?}

--- Configures `timeline`.
export type TimelineOptions = {clock: ClockId?}

--- Configures a nested `tweenRun` operation.
export type RunOptions = {
    mode: PlaybackMode?,
    count: integer?
}

--- Fires at address zero when an `emit` step or a timeline emit runs.
---
--- Observers watch this one definition and filter on the emission's `name`,
--- rather than each name being an event type of its own.
export const Event: events.Event<Emission> = sequencetypes.Event

--- The process-wide component a `tweenTrack` tracking source reads.
export const TrackingTarget = tweeneval.TrackingTargetComponent

--- The per-tick instruction budget a world gives a playback that asks for
--- none.
export const DEFAULT_BUDGET: integer = sequencecursors.DEFAULT_BUDGET

-- Who holds a pause when the caller does not say.
local DEFAULT_HOLDER: string = "user"

local stateKey: data.Key<sequencetypes.WorldState> = data.newKey("tecs.sequence.state", nil)

local function stateFor(exclusive world: ecs.World): sequencetypes.WorldState
    local existing = world.resources[stateKey]
    if existing ~= nil then
        return existing
    end
    local created = sequencecursors.newState()
    world.resources[stateKey] = created

    return created
end

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Authoring nodes
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

--- References an entity supplied through `PlayOptions.bindings`.
---
--- The reference resolves when the instruction using it runs rather than at
--- `play`, so a binding may name an entity that does not exist yet. A missing
--- binding resolves to nothing instead of faulting.
--- @param name the binding name, which must not be empty
--- @return a reference to put in a `call` or `emit` argument list, or to hand
---     to `playTween` or `await`
--- @raises when the name is empty
export function bind(name: string): EntityRef
    if name == "" then
        error("sequence.bind requires a non-empty binding name", 2)
    end

    return new sequencetypes.EntityRef(bindName = name, isBinding = true)
end

--- Builds a step that runs a registered action.
---
--- The action name resolves per world when the instruction runs, so a program
--- may name an action registered later. A name still unregistered when the
--- step runs faults the playback with `unregisteredAction`. The step costs no
--- tick.
--- @param action the registered action name
--- @param ... the constants the action receives, which are the same values for
---     every playback of this program; put anything that differs between two
---     playbacks in `PlayOptions.params` instead
--- @return a step for `define`, not to be put in a second program
--- @raises when the action name is empty
export function call(action: string, ...: any): Node
    if action == "" then
        error("sequence.call requires a non-empty action name", 2)
    end

    return new sequencetypes.Node(kind = "call", text = action, args = {...})
end

--- Builds a step that waits a duration in seconds.
---
--- The duration converts to whole ticks when the instruction runs, rounded to
--- nearest, and any non-zero duration waits at least one tick. Zero still costs
--- a tick: the cursor resumes on the program's next tick rather than carrying
--- on within this one.
--- @param seconds the non-negative duration
--- @return a step for `define`, not to be put in a second program
--- @raises when the duration is negative
export function wait(seconds: number): Node
    if seconds < 0 then
        error("sequence.wait requires a non-negative duration", 2)
    end

    return new sequencetypes.Node(kind = "wait", number = seconds)
end

--- Builds a step that waits a whole number of ticks of the program's clock.
--- @param steps the non-negative tick count, where zero yields until the next
---     tick
--- @return a step for `define`, not to be put in a second program
--- @raises when the count is negative or not a whole number
export function waitSteps(steps: integer): Node
    if steps < 0 or steps % 1 ~= 0 then
        error("sequence.waitSteps requires a non-negative whole number", 2)
    end

    return new sequencetypes.Node(kind = "waitSteps", number = steps)
end

--- Builds a step that blocks until `signal` raises a named signal.
---
--- The next fixed step delivers signals after `signal` runs, so a signal one
--- sequence raises never runs another within the same step. A signal raised
--- before the wait is not remembered.
--- @param name the signal name, which is registered nowhere
--- @return a step for `define`, not to be put in a second program
--- @raises when the name is empty
export function waitSignal(name: string): Node
    if name == "" then
        error("sequence.waitSignal requires a non-empty signal name", 2)
    end

    return new sequencetypes.Node(kind = "waitSignal", text = name)
end

--- Builds a step that blocks until a registered query matches or stops
--- matching.
---
--- Unlike a signal, which is an edge, this is a condition on the world: a wait
--- whose condition already holds resumes rather than waiting for a transition
--- that has already happened. The condition is evaluated at the start of a
--- fixed step rather than at the instruction, so a query wait costs at least
--- one step.
--- @param name the registered query name, resolved per world when the
---     instruction runs
--- @param condition `"any"` to wait for a match or `"empty"` to wait for none
--- @return a step for `define`, not to be put in a second program
--- @raises when the name is empty or the condition is neither value
export function waitQuery(name: string, condition: QueryCondition): Node
    if name == "" then
        error("sequence.waitQuery requires a non-empty query name", 2)
    end
    if condition ~= "any" and condition ~= "empty" then
        error("sequence.waitQuery condition must be 'any' or 'empty'", 2)
    end

    return new sequencetypes.Node(kind = "waitQuery", text = name, count = condition == "any" and 1 or 0)
end

--- Builds a step that emits a sequence event at address zero.
---
--- The step occupies no tick, so the step after it runs in the same one. Each
--- emission copies its argument list and sends it with the event, so an
--- observer may keep it, unlike the list an action receives.
--- @param event the name carried on the emission
--- @param ... the constants delivered on the emission with every entity
---     reference already resolved
--- @return a step for `define`, not to be put in a second program
--- @raises when the event name is empty
export function emit(event: string, ...: any): Node
    if event == "" then
        error("sequence.emit requires a non-empty event name", 2)
    end

    return new sequencetypes.Node(kind = "emit", text = event, args = {...})
end

--- Builds a step that repeats a block.
--- @param count the iterations, or nil to repeat until canceled
--- @param nodes the block, which must not be empty and which is run in order
---     and started again from its first node
--- @return a step for `define`, not to be put in a second program
--- @raises when the count is not a positive whole number, or the block is
---     empty
export function loop(count: integer?, nodes: {Node}): Node
    if count ~= nil and (count < 1 or count % 1 ~= 0) then
        error("sequence.loop count must be a positive whole number or nil", 2)
    end
    if #nodes == 0 then
        error("sequence.loop requires a non-empty block", 2)
    end

    return new sequencetypes.Node(kind = "loop", count = count, nodes = nodes)
end

--- Builds a step that starts a branch alongside the rest of the program.
---
--- The branch is a playback of its own running the same program at a different
--- instruction, inheriting the owner, bindings, parameters, and instruction
--- budget of the playback that forked it. It starts once that playback next
--- waits, joins, or ends, within the same tick. A branch never outlives its
--- parent, and a branch that faults takes its parent with it, faulted
--- `branchFaulted`, rather than leaving a join one branch short forever.
--- @param nodes the branch body, which must not be empty and which reads and
---     writes the forking playback's own bindings table rather than a copy
--- @return a step for `define`, not to be put in a second program
--- @raises when the block is empty
export function fork(nodes: {Node}): Node
    if #nodes == 0 then
        error("sequence.fork requires a non-empty block", 2)
    end

    return new sequencetypes.Node(kind = "fork", nodes = nodes)
end

--- Builds a step that waits for every branch not yet joined.
---
--- It falls straight through when there are none, and when the last branch
--- finishes the waiting playback resumes within that same tick.
--- @return a step for `define`, not to be put in a second program
export function join(): Node
    return new sequencetypes.Node(kind = "join")
end

--- Builds a step that forks several blocks and waits for all of them.
---
--- This is a `fork` per block followed by one `join`. Blocks start in argument
--- order, and each shares the playback's bindings table as a `fork` does.
--- @param blocks one non-empty block per branch
--- @return a step for `define`, not to be put in a second program
--- @raises when no block is supplied or one of them is empty
export function parallel(blocks: {{Node}}): Node
    if #blocks == 0 then
        error("sequence.parallel requires at least one block", 2)
    end
    for index = 1, #blocks do
        if #blocks[index] == 0 then
            error("sequence.parallel block " .. tostring(index) .. " is empty", 2)
        end
    end

    return new sequencetypes.Node(kind = "parallel", blocks = blocks)
end

--- Builds a step that plays a registered timeline on a bound entity.
---
--- The step costs no tick. It starts a separate playback owned by the bound
--- entity, running on the named program's clock and using this playback's
--- instruction budget. A binding that is missing or dead is not a fault:
--- nothing plays, and a following `waitTween` resumes at once reporting
--- `targetLost`.
--- @param timeline the timeline name, resolved when the instruction runs and
---     always to the newest version; an undefined name faults the playback
---     with `unregisteredTween`
--- @param target a `bind` reference and nothing else
--- @param params the started playback's parameters, so `mode`, `count`,
---     `speed`, `delay` and `channel` shape it as they do for `play`
--- @return a step for `define`, not to be put in a second program
--- @raises when the timeline name is empty or the target is not a `bind`
---     reference
export function playTween(timeline: string, target: EntityRef, params: {[string]: any}?): Node
    if timeline == "" then
        error("sequence.playTween requires a non-empty timeline name", 2)
    end
    if not sequenceprogram.isEntityRef(target) then
        error("sequence.playTween requires a sequence.bind(name) target", 2)
    end

    return new sequencetypes.Node(kind = "playTween", text = timeline, args = {target, params})
end

--- Builds a step that waits for the playback the most recent `playTween`
--- started.
---
--- It resumes when that specific playback completes, is canceled, loses its
--- channel, or loses its entity, so it never waits forever, and `status`
--- reports the outcome as `tweenOutcome`. A `waitTween` that no `playTween`
--- preceded falls straight through without costing a tick.
--- @return a step for `define`, not to be put in a second program
export function waitTween(): Node
    return new sequencetypes.Node(kind = "waitTween")
end

--- Builds a step that evaluates a registered evaluator every tick until it
--- finishes.
---
--- Unlike every other step this one does not hand the tick back: the playback
--- joins its clock's active set and the runtime steps it each tick. Values
--- that move every frame need this behavior.
--- @param evaluator the registered evaluator name, resolved when the
---     instruction runs; an unregistered name faults the playback with
---     `unregisteredEvaluator`
--- @param data the constant the evaluator receives, passed through its
---     `resolve` once per program constant and shared by every playback, so it
---     must remain plain data and the evaluator must not write per-playback
---     state into it
--- @return a step for `define`, not to be put in a second program
--- @raises when the evaluator name is empty
export function eval(evaluator: string, data: any): Node
    if evaluator == "" then
        error("sequence.eval requires a non-empty evaluator name", 2)
    end

    return new sequencetypes.Node(kind = "eval", text = evaluator, args = data)
end

--- Builds a step that waits for work outside the sequencer to finish.
---
--- A subsystem registers a provider under a name and answers whether the work
--- is still going; this parks the playback until it is not. The sequencer never
--- learns what the work is, which is how a program waits on a sprite animation
--- without the sequencer requiring the renderer. A binding that is missing or
--- dead is not a fault, and neither is a provider reporting work that never
--- started.
--- @param provider the registered provider name, checked before the binding; an
---     unregistered name faults the playback with `unregisteredAwaitable`
--- @param target a `bind` reference and nothing else
--- @param key handed to the provider unread, for a provider that answers about
---     more than one thing per entity
--- @return a step for `define`, not to be put in a second program
--- @raises when the provider name is empty or the target is not a `bind`
---     reference
export function await(provider: string, target: EntityRef, key: string?): Node
    if provider == "" then
        error("sequence.await requires a non-empty provider name", 2)
    end
    if not sequenceprogram.isEntityRef(target) then
        error("sequence.await requires a sequence.bind(name) target", 2)
    end

    return new sequencetypes.Node(kind = "await", text = provider, args = {target, key})
end

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Timeline authoring
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

--- Returns a built-in easing curve by name.
--- @param name the curve name
--- @return the curve, or nil when the name names no built-in
export function easing(name: EasingName): EasingFunction?
    return tweeneval.findEasing(name)
end

--- Interpolates one numeric field of a component.
--- @param component the component whose field moves
--- @param fieldName the field name
--- @return the target, which holds no per-playback state and is safe to share
export function targetField(component: ecs.Component, fieldName: string): Target
    return tweeneval.field(component, fieldName)
end

--- Interpolates two numeric fields of a component together.
--- @param component the component whose fields move
--- @param first the field the first destination lines up with
--- @param second the field the second destination lines up with
--- @return the target
export function targetField2(component: ecs.Component, first: string, second: string): Target
    return tweeneval.field2(component, first, second)
end

--- Interpolates one field as an angle, taking the short way round.
--- @param component the component whose field moves
--- @param fieldName the radian-valued field name
--- @return the target
export function targetAngle(component: ecs.Component, fieldName: string): Target
    return tweeneval.angle(component, fieldName)
end

--- Reads a moving destination from the animated entity itself.
--- @param component the component read
--- @param fields the fields read, lined up with the target's own
--- @return the source
export function sourceOwn(component: ecs.Component, fields: {string}): TrackSource
    return tweeneval.sourceOwn(component, fields)
end

--- Reads a moving destination from the live entity holding a world key.
--- Missing or despawned sources contribute zero until a live entity claims the key.
--- @param key the non-empty key resolved on every evaluation.
--- @param component the component to read from that entity.
--- @param fields the fields aligned with the target's fields.
--- @return the keyed tracking source.
--- @raises when the key is empty.
export function sourceKey(key: string, component: ecs.Component, fields: {string}): TrackSource
    return tweeneval.sourceKey(key, component, fields)
end

--- Reads a moving destination from the entity named by the animated entity's
--- `TrackingTarget`.
--- @param component the component read
--- @param fields the fields read, lined up with the target's own
--- @return the source
export function sourceTracking(component: ecs.Component, fields: {string}): TrackSource
    return tweeneval.sourceTracking(component, fields)
end

--- Reads a moving destination from the entity a relationship points at.
--- @param relationship the relationship traversed from the animated entity
--- @param component the component read on the target entity
--- @param fields the fields read, lined up with the target's own
--- @return the source
export function sourceRelated(relationship: ecs.Component, component: ecs.Component, fields: {string}): TrackSource
    return tweeneval.sourceRelated(relationship, component, fields)
end

--- Builds a timeline operation that interpolates to an absolute destination.
---
--- The operation reads its starting values from the entity on its first tick
--- rather than during compilation, so the same timeline played on two entities
--- starts from wherever each of them is.
--- @param duration the seconds it occupies, which must be greater than zero
--- @param curve a name from the built-in curves, or one of those curve values
--- @param target which component fields move and in which order the
---     destinations line up with them
--- @param t1 the destination for the target's first field, in that field's own
---     units: pixels for a translate, radians for a rotation, zero through one
---     for a color channel
--- @param t2 the destination for the second field
--- @param t3 the destination for the third field
--- @param t4 the destination for the fourth field, with anything past the
---     target's field count ignored and an omitted field treated as zero
--- @return an operation for a timeline spec
--- @raises when the curve names no built-in
export function tweenTo(
    duration: number,
    curve: EasingName | EasingFunction,
    target: TargetName | Target,
    t1: number,
    t2: number?,
    t3: number?,
    t4: number?
): TimelineNode
    return tweeneval.to(duration, curve, target, t1, t2, t3, t4)
end

--- Builds a timeline operation that interpolates by a delta from the starting
--- value.
--- @param duration the seconds it occupies, which must be greater than zero
--- @param curve a name from the built-in curves, or one of those curve values
--- @param target which component fields move and in which order
--- @param t1 the signed change applied to the target's first field
--- @param t2 the change applied to the second field
--- @param t3 the change applied to the third field
--- @param t4 the change applied to the fourth field
--- @return an operation for a timeline spec
--- @raises when the curve names no built-in
export function tweenAdjust(
    duration: number,
    curve: EasingName | EasingFunction,
    target: TargetName | Target,
    t1: number,
    t2: number?,
    t3: number?,
    t4: number?
): TimelineNode
    return tweeneval.adjust(duration, curve, target, t1, t2, t3, t4)
end

--- Builds a timeline operation that interpolates toward a destination that
--- keeps moving.
---
--- The first tick fixes the start as `tweenTo` does, and each later tick reads
--- the destination again while the operation remains inside its window. Once
--- the window ends the operation holds its last destination. A source whose
--- entity or component is missing reads as zero rather than faulting.
--- @param duration the seconds it occupies, which must be greater than zero
--- @param curve a name from the built-in curves, or one of those curve values
--- @param target which component fields move and which fields of the source
---     line up with them
--- @param from where the destination is read each tick
--- @return an operation for a timeline spec
--- @raises when the curve names no built-in
export function tweenTrack(
    duration: number,
    curve: EasingName | EasingFunction,
    target: TargetName | Target,
    from: TrackSource
): TimelineNode
    return tweeneval.track(duration, curve, target, from)
end

--- Builds a timeline operation that advances the cursor without changing
--- anything.
--- @param duration the seconds it occupies, where zero performs no work
--- @return an operation for a timeline spec
export function tweenWait(duration: number): TimelineNode
    return tweeneval.wait(duration)
end

--- Builds a timeline operation that emits a named sequence event.
---
--- It occupies no time, so what follows it starts at the same instant. The
--- emission carries the entity the timeline is animating as its one argument
--- and the timeline's own playback as its handle. It fires as the cursor
--- passes the point going forward, so a ping-pong return leg does not fire it
--- a second time.
--- @param name the name carried on the emission
--- @return an operation for a timeline spec
export function tweenEmit(name: string): TimelineNode
    return tweeneval.emit(name)
end

--- Builds a timeline operation that runs a nested timeline from its own spec.
---
--- The nested timeline occupies a window of the parent equal to its own length
--- times its pass count, so a repeating nested run needs a finite `count`. Its
--- operations keep their per-playback state separately from the parent's.
--- @param spec the nested operations, which this call reads rather than
---     rewrites
--- @param options omit to run the nested timeline once; `"loop"` and
---     `"pingPong"` need a `count` here
--- @return an operation for a timeline spec
--- @raises when a repeating nested run carries no finite count
export function tweenRun(spec: {TimelineNode}, options: RunOptions?): TimelineNode
    return tweeneval.run(spec, options and options.mode, options and options.count)
end

--- Builds a timeline operation that runs blocks concurrently and ends with the
--- longest.
---
--- Every block starts where the operation sits, and what follows starts after
--- the last of them ends. Two blocks writing the same component field is not
--- an error, and the later one in argument order wins each tick.
--- @param blocks one block per concurrent branch, each run in sequence within
---     itself
--- @return an operation for a timeline spec
export function tweenParallel(blocks: {{TimelineNode}}): TimelineNode
    return tweeneval.parallel(blocks)
end

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Compilation
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

--- Compiles a program under a stable symbolic name.
---
--- Defining over a name already in use publishes a new version rather than
--- replacing the old one: playbacks already running keep the version they
--- started on, and only later `play` calls see the new one.
--- @param name the stable symbolic name, which is a snapshot compatibility
---     surface
--- @param nodes the authored steps, which may be empty, in which case the
---     program completes on its first tick
--- @param options the clock the program's waits count, defaulting to `"fixed"`
--- @return the compiled program, immutable and shared by every playback of it,
---     so the same value plays on any number of worlds
--- @raises when the name is empty, the clock is not one of the three names, or
---     an entry is not a step
export function define(name: string, nodes: {Node}, options: DefineOptions?): Program
    if name == "" then
        error("sequence.define requires a non-empty program name", 2)
    end
    local clock = (options and options.clock) or "fixed"
    if clock ~= "fixed" and clock ~= "frame" and clock ~= "presentation" then
        error("sequence.define clock must be 'fixed', 'frame', or 'presentation'", 2)
    end
    local program = sequenceprogram.compile(name, sequenceregistry.nextVersion(name), clock, nodes)
    sequenceregistry.publish(program)

    return program
end

--- Compiles a tween timeline into a program under a stable name.
---
--- The compiled slots travel in the program's constant pool, so a timeline is
--- snapshot safe for the same reason every other program is. Play it with
--- `play`, giving the entity it animates as the `owner`: a playback with no
--- owner has nothing to write to and completes on its first tick.
---
--- A playback reads four parameters, all optional: `mode` is `"once"`,
--- `"loop"`, or `"pingPong"`; `count` is the passes a finite repeat makes;
--- `speed` is a playback multiplier defaulting to one; and `delay` is the
--- seconds to wait before the first frame. They are read when the playback
--- builds its state, so a speed of zero or less, a negative delay, or a repeat
--- of a zero-length timeline faults the playback with `actionError` rather
--- than raising from `play`.
--- @param name the stable symbolic name, sharing `define`'s namespace and
---     versioning
--- @param spec the timeline operations in execution order
--- @param options the clock the timeline is evaluated against, defaulting to
---     `"presentation"`; `"fixed"` makes it deterministic from a snapshot
---     alone at the cost of stepping at the simulation's rate
--- @return the compiled program, also reachable by name through `program`
--- @raises when the clock is neither `"fixed"` nor `"presentation"`, or an
---     operation is invalid
export function timeline(name: string, spec: {TimelineNode}, options: TimelineOptions?): Program
    local clock = (options and options.clock) or "presentation"
    if clock ~= "fixed" and clock ~= "presentation" then
        error("sequence.timeline clock must be 'fixed' or 'presentation'", 2)
    end
    local compiled = tweeneval.compile(spec)
    -- The serialized form is what the constant pool carries, and the evaluator
    -- resolves it back once when the first playback of it starts.

    return define(name, {eval(tweenrun.EVALUATOR, tweeneval.serializeTimeline(compiled))}, {clock = clock})
end

--- Returns the newest version of a defined program, or a requested version.
---
--- Every version a playback still runs stays reachable, and the newest version
--- of a name is never dropped.
--- @param name a name previously passed to `define` or `timeline`
--- @param version the version to look up, starting at one, or nil for the
---     newest
--- @return the program, or nil when the name was never defined or that version
---     has been dropped
export function program(name: string, version: integer?): Program?
    if version ~= nil then
        return sequenceregistry.version(name, version)
    end

    return sequenceregistry.newest(name)
end

--- Returns the names of every defined program, sorted.
--- @return a caller-owned list; registration is process-wide, so this spans
---     every world in the process
export function programNames(): {string}
    return sequenceregistry.names()
end

--- Renders a program as readable instructions.
--- @param value a program from `define` or `timeline`
--- @param pc the instruction to mark, as a playback's `status` reports it, or
---     nil for an unmarked listing
--- @return a header naming the program and its version, then one line per
---     instruction, newline separated, for reading rather than for parsing
export function disassemble(value: Program, pc: integer?): string
    return sequenceprogram.disassemble(value, pc)
end

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Registration
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

--- Registers an action a `call` step can name.
---
--- Actions must obey the deterministic-action contract to keep replay and
--- rewind meaningful: no wall-clock reads, no file or network access, and
--- randomness only from a generator whose state travels with the snapshot.
--- @param world the world that owns the action registry
--- @param name the action name, which is per world unlike an evaluator or an
---     awaitable; registering it twice replaces the action, and a playback
---     resolves the name at each `call`, so one already running picks the new
---     one up
--- @param action the body, which faults its playback with `actionError` when
---     it raises, leaving whatever it already wrote to the world written
--- @raises when the name is empty
export function registerAction(exclusive world: ecs.World, name: string, action: Action): nil
    if name == "" then
        error("sequence.registerAction requires a non-empty action name", 2)
    end
    stateFor(world).actions[name] = action
end

--- Reports whether a world has registered an action name.
--- @param world the world that owns the action registry
--- @param name the action name to find
--- @return false for a name this world never registered, including one another
---     world has
export function hasAction(exclusive world: ecs.World, name: string): boolean
    return stateFor(world).actions[name] ~= nil
end

--- Registers a query a `waitQuery` step can name.
---
--- Registering a name again re-tests every playback already parked on it,
--- against the new query, at the next fixed step.
--- @param world the world that owns the query registry
--- @param name the query name
--- @param descriptor the include and exclude constraints
--- @raises when the name is empty
export function registerQuery(exclusive world: ecs.World, name: string, descriptor: ecs.QueryDescriptor): nil
    if name == "" then
        error("sequence.registerQuery requires a non-empty query name", 2)
    end
    stateFor(world).queries[name] = world:newQuery(descriptor)
end

--- Reports whether a world has registered a query name.
--- @param world the world that owns the query registry
--- @param name the query name to find
--- @return false for a name this world never registered
export function hasQuery(exclusive world: ecs.World, name: string): boolean
    return stateFor(world).queries[name] ~= nil
end

--- Registers an evaluator an `eval` step can name.
---
--- Registration is process-wide, like a program: an evaluator is code, and two
--- worlds evaluating the same name run the same thing. Registering a name
--- twice replaces it.
--- @param name the evaluator name
--- @param evaluator the evaluator, which must carry `step`; without `save` and
---     `load` a playback evaluating this name comes back from a snapshot at the
---     start of its state rather than where it was
--- @raises when the name is empty or the evaluator has no `step`
export function registerEvaluator(name: string, evaluator: Evaluator): nil
    sequenceregistry.registerEvaluator(name, evaluator)
end

--- Registers a provider an `await` step can name.
---
--- Registration is process-wide, like an evaluator, because a provider is code.
--- Registering a name twice replaces the provider, so a playback already parked
--- on the name asks the new one at the next fixed step.
--- @param name the provider name
--- @param provider the provider, which must carry `isPending`; without
---     `setPaused` it leaves its work running when a pause stops the waiting
---     playback
--- @raises when the name is empty or the provider has no `isPending`
export function registerAwaitable(name: string, provider: Awaitable): nil
    sequenceregistry.registerAwaitable(name, provider)
end

--- Raises a named signal and wakes every playback blocked on it.
---
--- Delivery happens on the next fixed step. Raising a signal nothing waits on
--- is not an error and is not remembered: a playback that reaches `waitSignal`
--- afterwards keeps waiting.
--- @param world the world that owns the signal state
--- @param name the signal name, registered nowhere
--- @return the number of playbacks waiting on the name now, which may differ
---     from the set the next fixed step wakes
--- @raises when the name is empty
export function signal(exclusive world: ecs.World, name: string): integer
    if name == "" then
        error("sequence.signal requires a non-empty signal name", 2)
    end
    local state = stateFor(world)
    local waiting = #sequencecursors.parkedSlots(state.channels, name)
    -- Delivery is deferred to the next step so a signal raised from inside a
    -- running cursor cannot cascade within the same step.
    state.pendingSignals[#state.pendingSignals + 1] = name

    return waiting
end

--- Returns how many playbacks currently wait on a signal name.
--- @param world the world that owns the waiting playbacks
--- @param name the signal name to count
--- @return zero for a name nothing waits on; a signal raised during this step
---     leaves its waiters in the count until the next step delivers it
export function waitingOn(exclusive world: ecs.World, name: string): integer
    return #sequencecursors.parkedSlots(stateFor(world).channels, name)
end

--- Raises a signal whenever an event emits at address zero.
---
--- An ECS event is an edge, like a signal, so this wires one to the other
--- rather than adding a separate kind of wait. Delivery follows the ordinary
--- signal rule and waiters wake on the next fixed step.
--- @param world the world that owns the observer and the signal state
--- @param name the signal to raise, once per emission
--- @param event the event definition, observed at address zero and only there;
---     the observer lasts for the life of the world
--- @raises when the name is empty
export function signalOnEvent<T>(exclusive world: ecs.World, name: string, event: events.Event<T>): nil
    if name == "" then
        error("sequence.signalOnEvent requires a non-empty signal name", 2)
    end
    world:observe(0, event, function(_value: T, exclusive observing: ecs.World): nil
        signal(observing, name)
    end)
end

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Playback
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

--- Starts a program.
---
--- The first instruction runs on the next tick of the program's own clock,
--- never inside this call.
--- @param world the world that owns the new playback
--- @param value a program from `define` or `timeline`, not a name; the playback
---     runs this version for its whole life
--- @param options the owner, bindings, parameters, budget, and channel; taking
---     a channel another playback on the same owner holds cancels that one
---     first, reporting `replaced` to anything waiting on it
--- @return a handle to the new playback, carrying a generation so one whose
---     playback has ended never names a later playback that took the same slot
export function play(exclusive world: ecs.World, value: Program, options: PlayOptions?): Handle
    local state = stateFor(world)
    local cursor = sequencecursors.alloc(state, value, value.clock)
    sequenceregistry.retain(value)
    if options ~= nil then
        cursor.owner = options.owner or 0
        cursor.bindings = options.bindings
        cursor.params = options.params
        cursor.channel = options.channel
        local budget = options.budget
        if budget ~= nil then
            cursor.budget = budget
            cursor.budgetLeft = budget
        end
    end
    sequencecursors.linkOwner(state, cursor)
    sequencecursors.claimChannel(state, cursor)
    sequencecursors.schedule(state, cursor, sequencecursors.clockOf(state, cursor).step + 1)

    return sequencecursors.packHandle(cursor.slot, cursor.generation)
end

--- Stops a playback and releases its cursor.
---
--- This is safe on a finished handle. Cancelling takes the branches the
--- playback forked with it, and a playback parked in a `waitTween` on the one
--- canceled is told `canceled` and resumes. What a `playTween` started is a
--- playback in its own right and is not canceled with the one that started it,
--- unlike a pause, which cascades to it; stop it through its owner with
--- `cancelOwnedBy`.
--- @param world the world that owns the playback
--- @param handle the playback to stop, read against this world's cursor arena
--- @return false when the handle names nothing running, whether it already
---     finished or was already canceled
export function cancel(exclusive world: ecs.World, handle: Handle): boolean
    local state = stateFor(world)
    local cursor = sequencecursors.resolve(state, handle)
    if cursor == nil or not cursor.alive then
        return false
    end
    sequencecursors.retire(state, cursor, "canceled")

    return true
end

--- Suspends a playback, its branches, and anything it started.
---
--- Pause is holder counted: two systems can hold the same playback for
--- unrelated reasons, and it runs again only when the last one lets go, so
--- whichever resumes first cannot undo the other. Its wait, if any, resumes
--- from where it paused, and what it is waiting on stops with it when the
--- awaitable provider knows how.
--- @param world the world that owns the playback
--- @param handle the playback to pause
--- @param holder any string, treated as a set rather than a count, so two
---     pauses under the same holder are one hold; it defaults to `"user"`
--- @return whether the playback stopped, which is false when another holder
---     already had it or the handle names nothing running
export function pause(exclusive world: ecs.World, handle: Handle, holder: string?): boolean
    local state = stateFor(world)
    local cursor = sequencecursors.resolve(state, handle)
    if cursor == nil or not cursor.alive then
        return false
    end

    return sequencecursors.pauseTree(world, state, cursor, holder or DEFAULT_HOLDER)
end

--- Releases one holder's claim on a paused playback.
--- @param world the world that owns the playback
--- @param handle the paused playback
--- @param holder the string that took the hold, defaulting to `"user"`;
---     releasing one that never held it changes nothing
--- @return whether the playback started running again, which is true only for
---     the last holder to let go; a wake time that passed while it was held
---     runs it on the next tick rather than immediately
export function resume(exclusive world: ecs.World, handle: Handle, holder: string?): boolean
    local state = stateFor(world)
    local cursor = sequencecursors.resolve(state, handle)
    if cursor == nil or not cursor.alive then
        return false
    end

    return sequencecursors.resumeTree(world, state, cursor, holder or DEFAULT_HOLDER)
end

--- Returns a playback's status.
---
--- A finished playback still answers, with `state` saying how it ended, until
--- a later `play` takes its slot. The waiting fields are filled only while the
--- playback lives.
--- @param world the world that owns the playback
--- @param handle the playback to inspect, read against this world's cursor
---     arena
--- @return a caller-owned status, or nil for a handle this world never issued
export function status(exclusive world: ecs.World, handle: Handle): Status?
    local state = stateFor(world)
    local cursor = sequencecursors.resolve(state, handle)
    if cursor == nil then
        return nil
    end
    local branches: integer = 0
    local children = cursor.children
    if children ~= nil then
        for _ in pairs(children) do
            branches = branches + 1
        end
    end
    local alive = cursor.alive
    local condition: QueryCondition? = nil
    if alive and cursor.waitQuery ~= nil then
        condition = cursor.waitCondition == 1 and "any" or "empty"
    end

    return new sequencetypes.Status(
        state = cursor.state,
        program = cursor.program.name,
        version = cursor.program.version,
        pc = cursor.pc,
        wakeAt = alive and cursor.wakeAt or nil,
        waitingFor = alive and cursor.waitChannel or nil,
        waitingQuery = alive and cursor.waitQuery or nil,
        waitingCondition = condition,
        waitingAwaitable = alive and cursor.awaitProvider or nil,
        waitingAwaitableEntity = alive and cursor.awaitEntity or nil,
        waitingTween = cursor.waitingTween and cursor.tweenPlayback or nil,
        tweenOutcome = cursor.tweenOutcome,
        branches = branches,
        joining = cursor.joining,
        fault = cursor.fault,
        faultMessage = cursor.faultMessage
    )
end

--- Cancels every playback owned by an entity.
--- @param world the world that owns the playbacks
--- @param owner an entity id; nothing owns a playback started without an owner,
---     so no argument reaches one and only `cancel` on its handle stops it
--- @param reason what a playback parked in a `waitTween` on one of these is
---     told, defaulting to `canceled`
--- @return the number of canceled playbacks, counting each cursor once and
---     reaching branches through their inherited owner
export function cancelOwnedBy(exclusive world: ecs.World, owner: integer, reason: TweenOutcome?): integer
    local state = stateFor(world)
    local owned = state.ownerIndex[owner]
    if owned == nil then
        return 0
    end
    local slots: {integer} = {}
    for slot in pairs(owned) do
        slots[#slots + 1] = slot
    end
    table.sort(slots)
    local canceled: integer = 0
    for index = 1, #slots do
        local cursor = state.cursors[slots[index]]
        if cursor ~= nil and cursor.alive then
            sequencecursors.retire(state, cursor, "canceled", reason)
            canceled = canceled + 1
        end
    end

    return canceled
end

--- Returns the number of live playbacks, for tests and diagnostics.
--- @param world the world that owns the playbacks
--- @return the count, with branches and `playTween`-started playbacks counted
---     individually
export function activeCount(exclusive world: ecs.World): integer
    local state = stateFor(world)
    local count: integer = 0
    for _, cursor in pairs(state.cursors) do
        if cursor.alive then
            count = count + 1
        end
    end

    return count
end

--- Returns handles for every live playback in a stable order.
--- @param world the world that owns the playbacks
--- @return a caller-owned table ordered by cursor slot, which is stable within
---     a run but differs from creation order because new playbacks reuse free
---     slots
export function playbacks(exclusive world: ecs.World): {Handle}
    local state = stateFor(world)
    local slots: {integer} = {}
    for slot, cursor in pairs(state.cursors) do
        if cursor.alive then
            slots[#slots + 1] = slot
        end
    end
    table.sort(slots)
    local handles: {Handle} = {}
    for index = 1, #slots do
        local cursor = assert(state.cursors[slots[index]])
        handles[index] = sequencecursors.packHandle(cursor.slot, cursor.generation)
    end

    return handles
end

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Configuration and tooling
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

--- Sets the per-tick instruction budget for playbacks that ask for none.
---
--- The budget caps how many instructions one playback runs per tick, and
--- exceeding it faults the playback with `budgetExceeded` rather than deferring
--- the rest, so it guards against a loop with no wait in it rather than acting
--- as a scheduler. It applies to playbacks started after this call.
--- @param world the world the budget applies to
--- @param instructions the budget, which must be at least one
--- @raises when the budget is less than one
export function setInstructionBudget(exclusive world: ecs.World, instructions: integer): nil
    if instructions < 1 then
        error("sequence.setInstructionBudget requires a positive count", 2)
    end
    stateFor(world).budget = instructions
end

--- Returns a clock's current tick as counted by the sequencer.
--- @param world the world that owns the clock counters
--- @param clock which of the three counters to read, defaulting to `"fixed"`
--- @return the ticks of that one clock, counted from when this world's
---     sequencer state was created and comparable only against another reading
---     of the same clock
export function currentStep(exclusive world: ecs.World, clock: ClockId?): integer
    return sequencetypes.clockById(stateFor(world).clocks, clock or "fixed").step
end

--- Sets the nominal frame duration a `frame` or `presentation` wait converts
--- seconds against.
---
--- The nominal duration is used rather than a frame's measured time because a
--- wake is a tick number fixed when the wait runs and never revisited.
--- @param world the world whose future waits use this duration.
--- @param seconds the positive finite nominal duration in seconds.
--- @raises when the duration is not positive and finite.
export function setNominalFrameTime(exclusive world: ecs.World, seconds: number): nil
    world:setNominalFrameTime(seconds)
end

--- Returns the actions and emissions a playback will certainly perform next,
--- and their timing.
---
--- The walk covers the straight-line run ahead of where the playback sits,
--- accumulating waits, and stops at the first instruction whose successor
--- requires execution.
--- @param world the world that owns the playback
--- @param handle the playback to inspect
--- @param withinTicks report only what is due within this many ticks of the
---     playback's own clock, or nil for everything the walk can reach
--- @return a caller-owned list of the calls and emissions ahead, in order;
---     empty for a handle that names nothing running and for one parked on a
---     signal, a query, a join, a tween, or an evaluator, since none of those
---     has a predictable start. A wait written in seconds also ends the walk.
export function upcoming(exclusive world: ecs.World, handle: Handle, withinTicks: integer?): {Step}
    local state = stateFor(world)
    local cursor = sequencecursors.resolve(state, handle)
    if cursor == nil or not cursor.alive then
        return {}
    end
    local wakeAt = cursor.wakeAt
    if wakeAt == nil then
        return {}
    end
    local offset = wakeAt - sequencecursors.clockOf(state, cursor).step

    return sequenceprogram.ahead(cursor.program, cursor.pc, offset > 0 and offset or 0, withinTicks)
end

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Plugin
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

local installed: {[ecs.World]: boolean} = setmetatable({}, {__mode = "k"}) as {[ecs.World]: boolean}

--- Installs the sequencer into a world.
---
--- Installing twice on the same world does nothing, so a world that scripts
--- input may install the sequencer itself rather than relying on the
--- application having done it.
--- @param world the world receiving sequencer state, systems, and the snapshot
---     handler
export function plugin(exclusive world: ecs.World): nil
    if installed[world] then
        return
    end
    installed[world] = true
    local state = stateFor(world)
    -- Evaluators are process-wide, so this registers once however many worlds
    -- install the sequencer.
    tweenrun.install()

    -- These three system names are a compatibility surface: the debug server
    -- reports them and an agent selects on them.
    world:addSystem({
        name = "sequence.Advance",
        phase = ecs.phases.FixedFirst,
        run = function(dt: number, exclusive running: ecs.World): nil
            sequencevm.tick(running, state, "fixed", dt)
        end,
    })
    -- First is the earliest gameplay phase, ahead of the fixed group, so a
    -- frame-clock program has run before anything reads what it did. That is
    -- what scripted input needs and what the fixed clock cannot give it: a
    -- frame runs the fixed phases zero times or several, and a program driving
    -- input has to advance exactly once per frame either way.
    world:addSystem({
        name = "sequence.AdvanceFrame",
        phase = ecs.phases.First,
        run = function(dt: number, exclusive running: ecs.World): nil
            sequencevm.tick(running, state, "frame", dt)
        end,
    })
    -- Presentation runs in Update with the frame's real elapsed time: what it
    -- carries has to move at the display's rate, not the simulation's.
    world:addSystem({
        name = "sequence.AdvancePresentation",
        phase = ecs.phases.Update,
        run = function(dt: number, exclusive running: ecs.World): nil
            sequencevm.tick(running, state, "presentation", dt)
        end,
    })

    -- This snapshot handler key is a compatibility surface: a save file written
    -- by an earlier build names its sequencer state by this string, and a
    -- restore that finds no state under its key falls through to an empty
    -- sequencer rather than erroring.
    world:addSnapshotHandler({
        name = "tecs.sequence",
        save = function(exclusive _world: ecs.World): any
            return sequencesnapshot.save(state)
        end,
        load = function(exclusive _world: ecs.World, value: any): nil
            sequencesnapshot.load(state, value)
        end,
    })

    -- Owner lifetime: a despawned owner cancels the playbacks it governs.
    world:observe(0, ecs.OnDespawn, function(value: ecs.EntityLifecycle, exclusive despawning: ecs.World): nil
        if state.ownerIndex[value.entity] ~= nil then
            cancelOwnedBy(despawning, value.entity, "targetLost")
        end
    end)
end
